digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-55999-4
  rankdir="TB";
  "C_black_drawback" [label="(0,0,0) abductive-reasoning: The phenomenon of black phosphorus sheet instability and the knowledge of its semiconducting advantages lead to the hypothesis that black phosphorus has major stability drawbacks."];
  "C_black_synth" [label="(0,0,0) abductive-reasoning: The phenomenon of high-energy synthesis and the knowledge of conditions indicate that black phosphorus production is energy-intensive."];
  "C_host_limit" [label="(0,0,0) abductive-reasoning: The phenomenon of supramolecular/carbon hosts yielding amorphous materials and the knowledge of analysis limitations lead to the hypothesis that crystalline porous hosts are required for controllable polymerisation and structural determination."];
  "C_mof_ideal" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is MOFs with the listed properties, and if a host has those properties then it is ideal, so MOFs are ideal hosts for inclusion, polymerisation using visible light and structural analysis by diffraction of phosphorus chains."];
  "C_mol_unstable" [label="(0,0,0) inductive-reasoning: From the induction-case of low-nuclearity phosphorus species and the induction-common pattern, it follows that molecular forms of low nuclearity phosphorus species are unstable."];
  "I_common_mol" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Low-nuclearity molecular species of pnictogens are generally unstable under ambient conditions."];
  "I_common_red" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Red phosphorus is recognized as a stable allotrope with wide bandgap."];
  "I_know_amorphous" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Amorphous host materials prevent detailed structural analysis by diffraction methods."];
  "I_know_black_stab" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Monolayer black phosphorus (phosphorene) has tuneable bandgap and semiconducting properties but suffers from ambient instability."];
  "I_know_black_synth" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Black phosphorus synthesis requires extreme pressure and temperature conditions."];
  "I_mof_case" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently there is that metal-organic frameworks possess rich host–guest chemistry, tuneable porosity, high crystallinity and potential stability."];
  "I_problem_common" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Materials chemistry commonly seeks new polymeric forms to overcome existing allotrope and host limitations."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes and demonstrates that the helical channels of the indium-based MOF MFM-300(In) can confine white phosphorus, promote its polymerisation into double-helical chains of butterfly P8 units, stabilise those chains as phosphorus-doped MOF materials, and provide crystallographic evidence for P8-chain structural motifs relevant to red phosphorus while enabling discussion of tunable semiconducting properties.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A crystalline, porous and chemically robust host can bridge phosphorus-allotrope instability and structural-elucidation problems by confining reactive phosphorus species in ordered channels where polymerisation, stabilisation and diffraction-based characterisation become possible."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Taken together, the limitations of existing phosphorus forms and host systems support the materials-chemistry need for new stable, improved and structurally characterisable polymeric phosphorus forms."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) MOF nanochannels are a plausible host strategy for inclusion of white phosphorus, controllable polymerisation and stabilisation of phosphorus chains, and structural analysis by diffraction."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Red phosphorus is established here only as an important and stable phosphorus allotrope, more stable than black phosphorus, with a wide bandgap of up to 2.4 eV."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Induction-case: Phosphorus is remarkable for the structural diversity of its allotropes, but only a few of them are chemically stable and possess desired properties."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Supramolecular chemistry can promote controllable polymerisation and stabilisation of phosphorus chains within nanochannels of a porous host (covers CuI examples from sentence 13)."];
  "S14_S15" [label="(14,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Activated carbons and carbon nanotubes adsorb P4 and host polymerisation into chains, but due to amorphous nature their structure determination remains elusive."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Deduction-rule: If a host has rich host–guest chemistry, tuneable porosity, high crystallinity and potential stability, then it is ideal for inclusion of white phosphorus molecules which might be further polymerised using visible light and analysed structurally by diffraction (covers MOF advantages and potential doping from sentence 17)."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Induction-case: The molecular forms of low nuclearity species are unstable, with white phosphorus P4 self-igniting in air, and P2 and P6 species existing only in the gas phase at high temperature."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Sheets of black phosphorus are not stable towards a combination of moisture, oxygen and light (covers phosphorene semiconductor properties from sentence 4 via reference context)."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: High energy consumption is a drawback during the synthesis of black phosphorus from P4 at 1.3 GPa and 200 °C."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Induction-case: Red phosphorus is an important and stable allotrope, more stable than black phosphorus with a wide bandgap of up to 2.4 eV."];
  "S2" -> "C_mol_unstable" [label="induction-case"];
